# SQLite → PostgreSQL Portability

The app ships on SQLite for the single-user MVP but must migrate cleanly to PostgreSQL later (multi-user / web). Write all data-access code so the switch stays a one-line provider swap (`UseSqlite` → `UseNpgsql`) plus a `pgloader` data copy. Avoid the following.

## Queries & data access
- Don't write raw SQL (`FromSqlRaw`, `ExecuteSqlRaw`) with SQLite dialect — use LINQ/EF.
- Don't bypass EF with a direct SQLite connection (`Microsoft.Data.Sqlite` commands) in business code.
- Don't use SQLite-specific SQL functions (`datetime()`, `strftime()`, `json_extract()`, `GROUP_CONCAT`) — Postgres equivalents differ.
- Don't rely on SQLite's `LIKE` being case-insensitive — Postgres `LIKE` is case-sensitive (use `ILIKE`/`EF.Functions.ILike`).
- Don't leak the provider into UI/business layers — keep `UseSqlite`/`Npgsql` references only in the DataAccess layer.

## Types & schema design
- Don't lean on SQLite's loose typing — never store numbers/dates/bools in TEXT columns. Use proper `int`/`decimal`/`DateTime`/`bool`.
- Don't use local `DateTime.Now` casually — prefer `DateTime.UtcNow`; Postgres is strict about `timestamp` vs `timestamptz`.
- Don't depend on SQLite's forgiving `decimal` — set precision explicitly on money fields; Postgres enforces it.
- Don't assume booleans are 0/1 — use real `bool` and let EF map it.
- Don't hardcode SQLite-style identifiers/casing — let EF own table/column names so Postgres casing matches.

## EF configuration
- Don't hand-write raw SQL migrations — use Fluent API so config regenerates for Postgres.
- Don't use provider-specific column types via `HasColumnType("TEXT"/"INTEGER")` — let EF pick or use provider-agnostic mappings.
- Don't skip CHECK constraints in Fluent API — define them in code so they regenerate on Postgres.
- Don't seed data with SQLite-only tricks — use EF `HasData` or a provider-agnostic seeding service.

## Keys & identity
- Don't manually manage auto-increment IDs or assume SQLite's `rowid` behavior — let EF handle identity so Postgres sequences work.
- Don't reuse/recycle primary keys in app logic — it complicates sequence resets and FK integrity.

## General hygiene
- Don't scatter DB access across the app — funnel everything through repositories.
- Don't store file paths, connection strings, or `Data Source=...` assumptions in business logic — keep them in config.
- Don't test only against SQLite — once the schema stabilizes, run migrations against Postgres once to catch surprises early.

**Rule of thumb:** code against EF Core abstractions, use real .NET types, and keep all DB specifics in the DataAccess layer.
